TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN of 27.33° is described as indicating the launch facility location; RAAN is not set by launch site, and Kourou is near 53°W so the value is unjustified."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou (about 5°N) to a 97.8° retrograde orbit is an unusual, costly launch case; the report does not address azimuth or performance penalties."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Claim that two launch windows per day occur because site latitude is smaller than inclination is loosely reasoned and not supported by calculation."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E stated to first launch in 2024, yet the mission is planned for 2025 and the alternatives section is based on a 2021 calendar; timeline is inconsistent."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Altitude selection reasoning is contradictory: high altitude is said to give low resolution yet this is called desirable, and no swath or GSD calculation is shown."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Daily imaging of the whole reef is claimed at 600 km without a revisit or swath calculation; orbital period is not stated."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Only argument of latitude and five elements are given; the claim that Keplerian elements fully describe the orbit lacks epoch and state details."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"At 600 km, uncontrolled decay is not quantified against the 25-year guideline; claim of burn-up on uncontrolled descent is unsupported. Passive EOL is not resolved."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"States the only manoeuvre is at EOL, yet the EOL strategy is passive and undecided, so manoeuvre requirements are inconsistent."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table lists two entries numbered 13 and the text says fifteen periods, though numbering is inconsistent."}],"overall":"A reasonably developed sun-synchronous design with STK evidence and a clear introduction. Major weaknesses include inconsistent launch timeline, incorrect RAAN rationale, an unresolved and unquantified EOL, a truncated drag-sail sentence, and widespread citation failures including Wikipedia and missing references.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale and single-spacecraft choice are well argued with references. Users are vague: the operator is not clearly named, and customers are listed as possible agencies rather than specified. Some citations are weak or unsuitable, which limits the mark.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Other Earth observation satellites use networks or constellations of satellites"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"STK access results, ground track and inclination-altitude plot give reasonable evidence. However, RAAN is wrongly tied to launch site, the Vega E timeline conflicts with the 2025 launch, and the EOL strategy is unresolved and unquantified. A drag-sail sentence is broken, and no decay or 25-year compliance analysis is given.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The orbit is near-polar and retrograde, meaning the satellite will pass over almost the entire Earth"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"In this orbit, atmospheric drag should cause the satellite to completely burn up upon re-entry."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"It acts like a regular sail, utilising a large surface area to"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion restates the design and notes launch-vehicle risk with a fallback. It is largely a summary, however, and does not draw specific conclusions from the evidence, such as EOL outcome, duration adequacy or the contact-time results.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Headings and sections follow the required structure and the document is mostly readable. Presentation is weakened by a Wikipedia source, citations [49]–[74] absent from the bibliography, [10] used for two different sources, stray characters in references, a truncated sentence, unnumbered tables and figures, and a duplicated access number.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"It acts like a regular sail, utilising a large surface area to"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above."},{"where":"Launch","quote":"February (TBD)"}]}]}
